    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> kingdom built by the Dlamini dynasty under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobhuza-i">Sobhuza I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mswati-ii">Mswati II</a>, the king after whom the Swazi are named.</li><li>The king (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngwenyama">Ngwenyama</a>) rules alongside the queen mother (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndlovukati">Ndlovukati</a>), and kingship is renewed each year in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incwala">Incwala</a>.</li><li>After concessions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> and British rule, it regained independence in 1968 and remains one of the world’s last absolute monarchies.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobhuza-ii">Sobhuza II</a> reigned from 1899, when he was a few months old, until 1982, one of the longest reigns of any monarch in recorded history.</p></aside>
    </section>

